                "While classical Difference-in-Differences provides a powerful framework for paired market analysis, it possesses a severe vulnerability when applied to real-world corporate data: finding a single control market that perfectly mirrors a massive treatment metropolis is often impossible. For example, if an enterprise runs a high-stakes media blitz in London or New York City, no single other city (such as Manchester or Boston) exhibits identical economic scale, media consumption habits, and idiosyncratic retail dynamics. Pairing London with Manchester directly creates substantial model misspecification. To overcome this limitation, Alberto Abadie, Alexis Diamond, and Jens Hainmueller developed the Synthetic Control Method (SCM) in 2003, widely recognized by econometricians as one of the most important methodological innovations in policy evaluation and causal inference.",
                "The core philosophical insight of the Synthetic Control Method is that while no single control market can mirror the treatment market, a weighted combination of multiple unexposed control markets (a 'Synthetic Twin') can recreate the treatment market's historical trajectory with near-perfect fidelity. Consider an enterprise with one treated market (e.g., Texas) and a 'donor pool' of twenty untreated states (California, Florida, New York, Ohio, etc.). SCM formulates a constrained mathematical optimization problem that solves for a vector of non-negative weights W = [w_1, w_2, ..., w_J] assigned to each donor market in the donor pool, such that the weighted combination of donor markets minimizes the Mean Squared Prediction Error (MSPE) relative to the treated market across the pre-treatment period.",
